==English==
{{rfv}}

===Etymology===

A [[reverse]] [[spelling]] of ''[[palindrome|palindromes]]''. Macmillan  [http://www.macmillandictionary.com/new-words/031205-semordnilap.htm] suggest that British author Michael Quinion may have been among the first to use the word, in May 2000. However, an [http://www.anagrammy.com/anagrams/faq3.html anagrams FAQ page] attributes it to Lewis Carroll.

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A word, phrase, or sentence that has the property of forming another word, phrase, or sentence when its letters are reversed. A semordnilap differs from a [[palindrome]] in that the word or phrase resulting from the reversal must be different from the original word or phrase.
#: ''"desserts" and "stressed" are believed to be the longest common English '''semordnilaps'''.''
#: ''"diaper" and "repaid" are '''semordnilaps'''.''
#*{{quote-book|1965|[[w:Dmitri Borgmann|Dmitri Borgmann]]|Language on Vacation: An Olio of Orthological Oddities|page=42|url=http://books.google.com/books?id=OMpAAAAAIAAJ
|passage=[he] then goes on to credit us with giving him some of the best examples of
'''semordnilaps'''.}}
#*{{quote-book|2000|Gail Smith|Celebrate the Piano|page=18|pageurl=http://books.google.com/books?id=3gmlW_hW4JMC&pg=PA18
|passage=This song is a '''semordnilap'''.  It can be played forward and then sounds different when it is played backwards.}}
#*{{quote-book|2008|Alex Pogel & David Ozonoff|chapter=Contingency Structures and Concept Analysis|title=Formal Concept Analysis|editors=Medina & Obietkov|page=318|pageurl=http://books.google.com/books?id=J3mTaeAzEAAC&pg=PA318
|passage=ACE is an acronym for Analysis of Concepts for Epidemiologists, and a '''semordnilap''' for ECA, Epidemiological Concept Analysis. }}

